@@ -0,0 +1,182 @@
+# Ghost preview network traffic
+
+Ghost previews show every player where each builder is about to place a block. This page
+estimates their traffic before and after the rework, shows how the estimates were worked out,
+and lists what players will notice.
+
+These numbers are **estimates, not measurements**. They multiply per-message byte costs measured
+in Studio (listed below) by message rates. The rework has not been measured in a playtest yet.
+[How to check it](#how-to-check-the-estimate) says how.
+
+## What changed
+
+| | Before | After |
+|---|---|---|
+| Client to server | `MoveGhost` (`RemoteEvent`, reliable) fired every `RenderStepped`: 60 to 240+ a second, even when nothing changed | `GhostReport` (`UnreliableRemoteEvent`) fired only when the cell, block type, or visibility changes, at most 20 a second, plus one resend a second |
+| Payload up | Block type name (string) and cell (`Vector3`) | One 9-byte buffer: u16 sequence, u8 block type index, three i16 cell indices |
+| Server work | Moves a replicated ghost `Part` in `Workspace.Ghosts` on every message | Stores the latest state per player in a table. No ghost part exists on the server |
+| Server to clients | Property replication of each ghost part (`CFrame`, `Transparency`, ...), reliable, sharing the ordered stream with everything else | `GhostBatch` (`UnreliableRemoteEvent`): one buffer a tick, at most 20 a second, holding only the ghosts that changed, plus every ghost once a second |
+| Drawing | Every client renders the replicated parts, including your own ghost after a round trip | Each client draws the ghosts itself. Your own ghost moves in the same frame as your cursor. Other ghosts glide to each new cell |
+
+Code: `src/ReplicatedStorage/Shared/GhostCodec.luau` (wire format),
+`src/ServerScriptService/GhostPreview.server.luau`,
+`src/StarterPlayer/StarterPlayerScripts/GhostPreview.client.luau`. Block placement
+(`BlockPlacer`, `BlockPlacement.server.luau`, `PlaceBlock`) is unchanged.
+
+## Unit costs used
+
+Bytes per remote call, measured from a Studio playtest (from the `roblox-performance` skill's
+reference tables):
+
+| Item | Bytes |
+|---|---|
+| Remote call with no arguments | 9 |
+| `Vector3` argument | 13 (22 for a call holding only a `Vector3`) |
+| String argument | about 2 + length (a call holding a 10-character string is 21) |
+| Buffer argument | about 5 + length (a call holding a 200-byte buffer is 214) |
+| One replicated property change on an anchored part (`CFrame`) | 25 |
+| Several writes to one property in one server frame | replicate as one change |
+| Writing a property its current value | no change, nothing replicates |
+
+Rates:
+
+- **F**: a client's frame rate. 60 is common; 144 and 240 happen on gaming PCs.
+- **c**: how many times a second a builder's cursor cell changes. A 4-stud grid swept by the
+  mouse, a turning camera, or a walking character changes cells often. These estimates use
+  c = 20 for someone actively building and c = 0 for someone idle. The server can replicate a
+  change at most once a frame, so before the rework c is capped at 60 on the way down.
+- **N**: players, 12. Every player's ghost is shown by default (`BlockPlacer` starts enabled),
+  so all N players have a ghost.
+
+## Before
+
+### Client to server
+
+One `MoveGhost(name, cell)` call: 9 + (2 + 5 for "Brick") + 13 ≈ **30 bytes**. It is fired every
+frame whether or not anything changed.
+
+| Frame rate F | Calls a second per client | Upload per client | Server receives from 12 clients |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| 60 | 60 | 30 × 60 = 1.8 KB/s | 720 calls/s, 21.6 KB/s |
+| 144 | 144 | 4.3 KB/s | 1,728 calls/s, 52 KB/s |
+| 240 | 240 | 7.2 KB/s | 2,880 calls/s, 86 KB/s |
+
+Each call is a reliable remote: it counts against the roughly 500 calls a second a client may
+send across all its `RemoteEvent`s (excess calls are delayed, not dropped), and it shares the
+ordered reliable stream with `PlaceBlock`. A 240 FPS client spends half that allowance on
+ghosts.
+
+### Server to each client
+
+Each call moves a replicated part. Writing the same `CFrame` again replicates nothing, so a ghost
+costs about 25 bytes for each server frame in which its cell changed, sent to every client
+(including its owner):
+
+- Active building, c = 20: 12 ghosts × 20 × 25 = **6 KB/s per client**, 72 KB/s out of the
+  server for 12 clients.
+- Worst case, every ghost changes cell every server frame (c = 60): 12 × 60 × 25 =
+  **18 KB/s per client**, 216 KB/s out of the server.
+
+Replicated properties are sent reliably, first in, first out, with no priority. A burst of ghost
+`CFrame` changes queues ahead of block placements and everything else, which is how data ping
+climbs to 300 to 400 ms. When a queue drains, several stale ghost positions arrive together and
+the ghost jumps.
+
+## After
+
+### Client to server
+
+One `GhostReport(buffer)` call: 9 + 5 + 9-byte buffer ≈ **23 bytes**.
+
+It is sent when the state differs from the last one sent and at least 50 ms have passed, so at
+most 20 a second, and resent once a second while nothing changes (an unreliable packet can be
+lost). Frame rate no longer matters.
+
+| Situation | Calls a second per client | Upload per client | Server receives from 12 clients |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Actively building (c ≥ 20) | 20 | 23 × 20 = 460 B/s | 240 calls/s, 5.5 KB/s |
+| Idle | 1 | 23 B/s | 12 calls/s, 0.3 KB/s |
+
+Unreliable remotes have their own call allowance, separate from `PlaceBlock`'s, and never hold
+up the reliable stream. The server ignores reports beyond 30 a second from one player, so a
+modified client cannot make it do more work than about 30 decodes a second.
+
+### Server to each client
+
+The server sends one `GhostBatch` per tick (every 50 ms) holding only the ghosts that changed,
+and once a second every ghost. One batch: 9 + 5 + 4-byte header + 15 bytes per ghost (8-byte
+UserId, 7-byte state).
+
+- Active building, all 12 changing every tick: 20 × (18 + 12 × 15) = 20 × 198 =
+  **4.0 KB/s per client**, 48 KB/s out of the server. This is also the ceiling: the batch rate
+  is fixed at 20 a second, so neither frame rate nor a misbehaving client can push it higher.
+- Half the builders active (6 changing per tick): 20 × (18 + 6 × 15) = **2.2 KB/s per client**.
+- Nobody moving: only the once-a-second refresh, 18 + 12 × 15 = **0.2 KB/s per client**.
+
+Above 60 players a batch is split so each buffer stays under the 1,000-byte limit of an
+unreliable payload.
+
+### Summary for 12 players
+
+| | Before (60 FPS) | Before (240 FPS) | After |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Upload per client, building | 1.8 KB/s | 7.2 KB/s | 0.46 KB/s |
+| Remote calls the server handles each second | 720 | 2,880 | ≤ 240 in, ≤ 240 out (20 batches × 12 clients) |
+| Download per client, 12 building (c = 20) | 6 KB/s | 6 KB/s | 4.0 KB/s |
+| Download per client, worst case | 18 KB/s | 18 KB/s | 4.0 KB/s |
+| Download per client, nobody moving | about 0 | about 0 | 0.2 KB/s |
+| Ghost traffic on the reliable stream | all of it | all of it | none |
+| Ghost part updates on the server each second (4 property writes each) | 720 | 2,880 | 0 |
+
+The download saving is moderate at typical cursor speeds (6 to 4 KB/s) and large in the worst
+case (18 to 4 KB/s, and capped). The bigger wins are the upload, which drops 4x to 16x and no
+longer grows with frame rate, the 3x to 12x fewer messages the server handles, and taking the
+ghosts off the reliable stream so placements and other replication no longer queue behind
+them. Ghost traffic now grows by about 15 bytes × 20 a second = 0.3 KB/s per client for each
+extra active builder, so 24 players cost about 8 KB/s per client.
+
+The one place the rework costs more is an idle server: the once-a-second refresh adds about
+0.2 KB/s per client where an idle server used to replicate nothing (though idle clients used to
+upload 30 bytes every frame regardless).
+
+## What players will notice
+
+- **Your own ghost is instant.** It moves in the same frame as your cursor. Before, it waited a
+  full round trip to the server and back, plus any queue.
+- **Other players' ghosts glide between cells** instead of snapping. They cover most of the
+  distance to a new cell in about 40 ms, so they read as smooth movement rather than 20 steps a
+  second. A jump longer than 8 cells (32 studs), or a ghost that was hidden and reappears,
+  snaps straight to its cell.
+- **Other players' ghosts lag a little behind their cursor**: up to 50 ms waiting for the
+  builder's next report slot, up to 50 ms waiting for the server's next batch, then the one-way
+  network delay and the glide. On a healthy server that is about 100 to 200 ms in all, steady,
+  instead of the 300 to 400 ms and jumps seen in the test.
+- **A dropped packet can leave a ghost one cell behind for up to a second**, until the next
+  refresh or report fixes it. This is rare and usually unnoticeable, because the next change
+  replaces it anyway.
+- **A player who just joined sees existing ghosts within about a second**, on the next refresh.
+- **Ghost parts exist only on clients.** `Workspace.Ghosts` is now a folder each client makes
+  for itself, and the server has no ghost parts. Nothing in this repo read the server's ghost
+  parts.
+- A ghost more than about 131,000 studs from the origin is hidden (the cell index no longer fits
+  in 16 bits). Players cannot reach that far in this place.
+
+## How to check the estimate
+
+Run the same workload before and after: a local server with as many clients as Studio allows,
+every client holding a building cursor and sweeping it across the baseplate.
+
+1. On each client, read `Stats.DataSendKbps` and `Stats.DataReceiveKbps` (or the Network tab of
+   the Developer Console) for 30 seconds while building, and again while idle.
+2. On the server, read `Stats.DataSendKbps` and `Stats.DataReceiveKbps` for the same windows.
+3. Compare with the tables above. Data ping (Performance Stats bar) staying close to network ping
+   while everyone builds shows that the reliable queue no longer backs up.
+4. In a live 12-player server, ask testers for their Performance Stats ping while several people
+   build, the number that was 300 to 400 ms before.
+
+## Further levers, not taken
+
+- Leave the builder's own entry out of the batch it receives, saving 15 bytes a tick per client
+  (about 0.3 KB/s). It needs a separate `FireClient` per player.
+- Send each ghost only to players within some distance of it, or at a lower rate far away.
+  Worth it at much larger player counts or map sizes.
